WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:07.282
Down the street from the corner shop is the print shop, with a big photocopier.

2
00:00:07.282 --> 00:00:12.271
Fill in a form once, copy it, change two lines on each copy, and you've saved an hour.

3
00:00:12.500 --> 00:00:15.475
The corner shop does the same thing every week.

4
00:00:15.475 --> 00:00:21.996
The Saturday breakfast box is set up once, and each customer gets a copy with their own name and notes.

5
00:00:21.996 --> 00:00:26.237
Tests do it too: one sample order, copied and changed in each test.

6
00:00:27.167 --> 00:00:30.887
In Kotlin, a data class gives you copy for free.

7
00:00:30.887 --> 00:00:34.375
Here's a cart with an id and a list of items.

8
00:00:34.375 --> 00:00:35.538
c1 holds bread.

9
00:00:35.538 --> 00:00:38.173
c2 is a copy of c1, with a new id.

10
00:00:38.173 --> 00:00:40.576
Then we add milk to c2's items.

11
00:00:40.800 --> 00:00:43.951
Here's a question we're all a little shy to ask.

12
00:00:43.951 --> 00:00:45.461
copy made a new object.

13
00:00:45.461 --> 00:00:48.152
So the two carts are separate now, right?

14
00:00:48.152 --> 00:00:49.531
What's in c1's items?

15
00:00:52.700 --> 00:00:59.128
The lesson's CopyTrap demo printed: c1.items after changing c2: bread, milk.

16
00:00:59.333 --> 00:00:59.746
Well.

17
00:00:59.746 --> 00:01:02.057
Nobody put milk in cart one.

18
00:01:02.057 --> 00:01:03.791
And yet, there it is.

19
00:01:04.700 --> 00:01:06.459
Let's look one level down.

20
00:01:06.459 --> 00:01:10.520
A field that holds an object doesn't hold the object itself.

21
00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:15.189
It holds a reference: an arrow that points to where the object lives.

22
00:01:15.189 --> 00:01:18.167
So c1's items field is an arrow to one list.

23
00:01:18.400 --> 00:01:22.168
copy makes a new cart, and copies each field's value.

24
00:01:22.168 --> 00:01:25.297
The id is a plain value, so c2 gets its own.

25
00:01:25.297 --> 00:01:27.785
But the value in items is an arrow.

26
00:01:27.785 --> 00:01:30.558
So copy copies the arrow, not the list.

27
00:01:30.558 --> 00:01:31.980
Two carts, one list.

28
00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:33.381
Now c2 adds milk.

29
00:01:33.381 --> 00:01:37.478
It follows its arrow, and writes on the only list there is.

30
00:01:37.478 --> 00:01:41.159
c1 follows its arrow to the same list, and sees milk.

31
00:01:41.367 --> 00:01:43.311
This is called a shallow copy.

32
00:01:43.311 --> 00:01:49.402
Each field's value is copied, and when the value is a reference, only the reference is copied.

33
00:01:49.402 --> 00:01:53.291
The Kotlin docs say it plainly: copy creates a shallow copy.

34
00:01:53.500 --> 00:01:57.496
If copy had an honest name, it would be called copyTheArrows.

35
00:01:57.496 --> 00:01:58.740
It's very obliging.

36
00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:02.736
It copies exactly what you asked for, and not one thing more.

37
00:02:03.667 --> 00:02:06.074
And here the photocopier picture breaks.

38
00:02:06.074 --> 00:02:07.999
A photocopy is a separate sheet.

39
00:02:07.999 --> 00:02:10.466
Writing on it never changes the original.

40
00:02:10.466 --> 00:02:13.715
But picture a form with a shopping list clipped to it.

41
00:02:13.715 --> 00:02:17.687
copy gives you a new form, with the same shopping list clipped on.

42
00:02:17.687 --> 00:02:21.477
Write on the copy's list, and you've written on the original's.

43
00:02:22.400 --> 00:02:28.503
Making a new object by copying one and changing a few fields is the pattern called Prototype.

44
00:02:28.503 --> 00:02:33.293
And now you know its catch: every copy is shallow until you make it safe.

45
00:02:34.200 --> 00:02:35.690
Java is no different.

46
00:02:35.690 --> 00:02:42.714
A record's wither, a method that returns a copy with one field changed, passes the same list along.

47
00:02:42.714 --> 00:02:48.390
And Java's original tool, Cloneable, is an interface with no clone method in it.

48
00:02:48.390 --> 00:02:50.519
A promise with nothing inside.

49
00:02:50.519 --> 00:02:51.087
Skip it.

50
00:02:52.000 --> 00:02:54.649
The fix is the same in both languages.

51
00:02:54.649 --> 00:03:02.804
Store a list that can't change: a read-only List in Kotlin, and List.copyOf in the Java record's compact constructor.

52
00:03:02.804 --> 00:03:07.892
To add milk, build a new list: copy, with items equal to items plus milk.

53
00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:09.993
Watch the arrows now.

54
00:03:09.993 --> 00:03:12.711
c2 gets a brand new list, bread and milk.

55
00:03:12.711 --> 00:03:15.629
c1 still points at its own list, just bread.

56
00:03:15.629 --> 00:03:21.000
And sharing a list is harmless now, because nobody can write on the shared sheet.

57
00:03:21.233 --> 00:03:22.473
So here's the rule.

58
00:03:22.473 --> 00:03:25.476
A copy is only as safe as the parts it shares.

59
00:03:25.476 --> 00:03:27.760
Share only parts that can't change.

60
00:03:28.667 --> 00:03:30.742
Kotlin has one more copy trap.

61
00:03:30.742 --> 00:03:36.899
Say Email has a private constructor, and a factory, Email.of, that checks for an at sign.

62
00:03:36.899 --> 00:03:39.320
The generated copy is still public.

63
00:03:39.320 --> 00:03:44.992
So copy, with the value not-an-email, compiles, and walks right past your factory.

64
00:03:45.200 --> 00:03:48.558
The demo printed: copy() skipped the factory.

65
00:03:48.558 --> 00:03:56.840
Kotlin 2.4 only warns, at the line that calls copy, and says this will become an error in language version 2.5.

66
00:03:57.767 --> 00:03:58.468
Two fixes.

67
00:03:58.468 --> 00:04:03.868
Add ConsistentCopyVisibility, and copy becomes as private as the constructor.

68
00:04:03.868 --> 00:04:08.777
Or, safer still, put the check in an init block, because copy runs it.

69
00:04:08.777 --> 00:04:16.140
The demo's Price checks for a negative number there, and copy with minus one printed: negative price: -1.

70
00:04:17.067 --> 00:04:19.744
So, is a copy separate from the original?

71
00:04:19.744 --> 00:04:21.637
Only as far as its fields go.

72
00:04:21.637 --> 00:04:23.204
Plain values are copied.

73
00:04:23.204 --> 00:04:25.033
Arrows are copied as arrows.

74
00:04:25.033 --> 00:04:30.583
Make what's shared unchangeable, keep your checks in init, and copies are safe again.

75
00:04:30.800 --> 00:04:31.611
Quick check.

76
00:04:31.611 --> 00:04:35.395
The breakfast box template holds a mutable list of tags.

77
00:04:35.395 --> 00:04:38.908
Anna's copy gets the tag no nuts, added to its list.

78
00:04:38.908 --> 00:04:41.813
What do Ben's box and the template now say?

79
00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:48.864
No nuts, both of them, because all three share one list.

80
00:04:48.864 --> 00:04:54.315
With a read-only list and copy with tags plus no nuts, only Anna's box changes.
